"Every culture has its own belief system about health and illness.": "afb213da2665",
"Finally, it's worth explaining why doctors bother with the system at all: \"The real use is noticing patterns across seemingly unrelated symptoms — irritability, headaches, and digestive trouble showing up together, for example, all pointing back to one element rather than being three separate coincidences. It's a diagnostic tool, not a belief system, and it still ends the same way every lesson in this course does: for anything real, see an actual doctor.\"": "d341578005a3",
"From there, it helps to give one concrete example rather than listing all five: \"Take 'wood.' It's associated with growth and upward movement, so it's matched with the liver, the emotion of anger, and springtime — not because they share a material, but because they share a pattern of pushing forward and expanding. When a doctor says stress is 'affecting your wood element,' they mean it's showing up in that whole family of related functions, not that there's actual wood somewhere in your body.\" This keeps the abstraction tied to something the friend can picture.": "d9e46d7f6ea3",
